  "subtitle": "The first handheld cellular phone — the whole device is the handset, and every call is a dial-then-Send ritual with no separate earpiece.",
  "why_scout_cares": "This is the museum's first cellular/mobile-phone artifact and the foundation of the modern mobile call gesture. Unlike any landline handset, the DynaTAC has NO separate handset — the entire device IS the handset, with a single-line LED display as the dialing buffer. The user types a number into the LED readout, then must press a dedicated 'Snd' (Send) key to actually seize a channel and connect: a modal two-phase dial-then-Send gesture that is the direct ancestor of every mobile 'green call button' on Earth. Beyond the standard 12-key pad, it carries nine special keys (Rcl/recall, Clr/clear, Snd/send, Sto/store, Fcn/function, End, Pwr/power, Lock, Vol/volume) and a 30-number store-and-recall memory. The interaction model — type into a buffer, then commit — is the ancestor of countless modal commit gestures in mobile and embedded computing. Fills the entirely empty cellular-HCI category and extends the museum's telephony narrative (Novation CAT, Hayes Smartmodem, Minitel, TI Silent 700, ROLM CBX) to the birth of the portable cellular handset.",
  "search_terms": ["Motorola DynaTAC", "DynaTAC 8000X", "first cellular phone", "dial then send", "Snd key", "Martin Cooper", "brick phone", "AMPS handheld"],
  "research": {
    "title": "Motorola DynaTAC 8000X",
